//OUT="3 2 1 done"

// A recursive call reuses the instance being built only once that instance's
// return type is known (`flush_instance_cache` in src/compiler/normalizer).
// Without a hint the type is pinned by the `ret` statements as the body is
// normalized, and for a function with no `ret` at all it is settled as void
// only after the whole body. So a recursive call reached before any `ret`
// normalizes the function again and again, until the compile fails with "This
// function is in the 100-th recursive call in the normalization phase".
//
// A void function has no `ret` to put first, so recursing like this needs the
// hint: with `-> void` the return type is known from the signature. The
// alternative is a base case that returns early, above the recursive call,
// which is what 04_recursion.cpl does. 41 is the same ordering with an i32
// return type.

fn countdown n: i32 -> void
    if n > 0
        out "{n} "
        countdown(n - 1)

fn main
    countdown(3)
    out "done"
